EXAMPLES = [
("write me a one paragraph story about a lighthouse keeper",
"""For thirty-one years Ada kept the light on Gannet Rock, and in all that time she never saw a ship come to harm, which the inspectors took as proof of her diligence and she took as proof of nothing at all. Ships, she knew, mostly stayed off the rocks because their captains could read a chart. Still, every evening she climbed the hundred and twelve steps, trimmed the wick, and wound the clockwork, because the one night she didn't would be the night it mattered. When the coast guard finally automated the station, a technician installed a sensor that did her whole job in a box the size of a bread loaf. Ada watched it blink on at dusk, perfectly on time, and found she could not go to bed until she had seen it do so twice."""),

("write a short story about a robot that learns to garden",
"""The robot had been built to sort packages, and when the warehouse closed it was left behind in the lot with nothing to sort. In spring, a seed blew into a crack in the asphalt beside it, and a small green thing came up. The robot watched it for eleven days. It had no instructions for this. On the twelfth day the weeds around the seedling grew tall enough to shade it, and the robot, after considerable deliberation, sorted them: weeds to one pile, seedling left in place. That evening it rained, and the robot recorded the event as a delivery. By midsummer the lot held tomatoes, a sunflower taller than the robot itself, and a neat row of beans the robot had planted from seeds it found in a discarded salad. People began to stop at the fence. One left a watering can. The robot logged it, gratefully, as equipment."""),

("describe a thunderstorm from a cat's point of view",
"""The air went wrong first, heavy and humming, the way it does before the large human drops a pan. Then the window light went the color of old milk, and the birds stopped talking all at once, which is never a good sign. When the first crack came, I was already under the bed, where the floor is honest and nothing can reach me but the dust. The sky kept tearing open overhead. The humans walked around as if nothing were happening, drinking their hot water and saying my name in soft, ridiculous voices, and I understood once again that they do not know anything. Later, when the rain softened to a pattering, I came out and sat in the window as though I had been there all along, and I watched the wet world with the calm of a creature who had personally ended the storm."""),

("write a horror story in five sentences",
"""The baby monitor crackled at 3 a.m., and through the static came a soft voice singing the lullaby my mother used to sing. I lay there listening, comforted for a moment, until I remembered that my mother had died in the spring, and that no one else knew that song. I ran down the hall and threw open the nursery door. My daughter was asleep in her crib, alone, breathing slow and even, and the room was perfectly still. Behind me, from the monitor I had left on my pillow, the voice said quietly, "You shouldn't have left her in here with me." """),

("write a letter to my future self",
"""Dear future me,

By the time you read this, you'll know how the things I'm worried about turned out, which puts you at an unfair advantage. I hope most of them turned out to be smaller than they felt. I hope you kept the habits that were good for us, like the long walks and the Sunday phone calls, and quietly dropped the ones that weren't. I hope you're still curious, still a little stubborn, and still capable of being delighted by an unexpected kindness. If things are harder than I imagined, I want you to know that I believed in you, from back here, without knowing anything about what you'd face. And if things are wonderful, enjoy them without guilt. You earned that, partly on my behalf.

With love and some envy,
Me"""),

("write a bedtime story about a sleepy dragon",
"""In the high mountains, where the clouds sleep on the peaks, there lived a dragon named Ember who was very, very bad at staying awake. Other dragons guarded their gold all night with blazing eyes. Ember tried. She curled around her small pile of coins and buttons, which was mostly buttons, and she watched the cave entrance with great determination. But the stars came out one by one, and the wind hummed through the rocks like someone singing low, and her eyelids grew as heavy as anvils. "Just a little rest," she said, and laid her chin on her tail. Her breath slowed to a warm, gentle puff, puff, puff, and each puff made a tiny orange glow that lit the cave like a nightlight. Down in the valley, the village children saw the soft light flickering high on the mountain and felt safe, because they knew that up there, Ember was dreaming. And now it's time for you to dream too."""),

("describe the smell of rain",
"""Before the rain, the air smells of hot stone and expectation, a metallic tang like the inside of a coin purse. Then the first drops strike dry ground and release something older: an earthy, green, almost sweet scent rising from the soil as if the ground had been holding its breath all summer and finally let it out. On city pavement it mixes with dust and tar and becomes sharper; in a forest it turns soft and loamy. There's a word for it, petrichor, but the word is too tidy for the thing, which smells less like a chemical reaction than like a promise being kept."""),

("start a story with the line 'The last train left without her'",
"""The last train left without her, and Mira stood on the platform watching its red lights shrink into the dark until they were indistinguishable from the stars. She had run the whole length of the station, her suitcase banging against her knees, and arrived exactly in time to see the doors close. Now the station was empty except for a man in a heavy coat sweeping the far end of the platform with the slow patience of someone who has seen many people miss many trains. "Next one's at six," he called, without looking up. Mira sat down on her suitcase. Six hours. It occurred to her, with a strange lightness, that for the first time in years nobody knew where she was, and nobody needed her to be anywhere until morning."""),

("help me write a breakup text that's kind but clear",
"""Here's a version you can adjust to your own voice:

"Hi Sam. I've been thinking a lot about us, and I've realized I don't feel the way I should to keep going in this relationship. You've been nothing but good to me, and that makes this harder to say, but I'd rather be honest than keep pretending. I'm ending things. I'm really grateful for the time we had, and I'm sorry to be the one causing hurt. I hope you'll take care of yourself."

If you've been together a long time, consider having this conversation in person or by phone instead; a text is fine for something new or brief, but a longer relationship usually deserves your voice."""),

("write a short toast for my best friend's wedding",
"""Good evening, everyone. I'm Jess, and I've had the privilege of being Maya's best friend since we were eleven, when she lent me her favorite pen and then made me promise to return it before lunch. I did, and she's trusted me ever since.

Maya has always been the person who notices: who remembers the name of your childhood dog, who shows up with soup before you've told anyone you're sick. For years, I wondered who would ever notice her that well. Then she met Daniel, and the first time I saw him hand her a sweater before she realized she was cold, I knew.

Daniel, Maya, may you keep noticing each other, in the small things and the large ones, for the rest of your lives. Please raise your glasses: to Maya and Daniel."""),

("describe a cozy bookstore",
"""The shop sits a few steps below the street, so you enter by going down, which feels right, like settling into a chair. Inside, the ceiling is low and the shelves run all the way up it, crammed past capacity, with paperbacks laid sideways on top of the rows. The floorboards creak in particular places that regulars learn to step around. It smells of paper, dust, and the coffee the owner keeps on a hot plate behind the counter, and somewhere in the back an old radiator ticks. A cat asleep on the poetry shelf has been there long enough that nobody remembers which customer first asked about it. There are two armchairs, both slightly too soft, and in the afternoon they're always taken by people who came in to buy one book and have not yet decided which."""),

("write a short fable about a fox and a crow, but with a twist",
"""A crow sat in a tree with a piece of cheese, and a fox came by and began to flatter her. "What glossy feathers," said the fox. "What a graceful neck. Surely a bird so beautiful must have a voice to match." The crow had heard this story before, as all crows have. She put the cheese carefully under her foot and said, "Thank you. You have a lovely voice yourself. Won't you sing for me?" The fox, who had never been asked, was so pleased that he threw back his head and howled a long, quavering note. The crow listened politely until he finished. Then she ate the cheese, and the fox went home hungry but strangely happy, and he came back the next day to sing again.

Moral: Flattery works on everyone, including the flatterer."""),

("write a haiku about autumn",
"""Rake set down at dusk—
the wind returns every leaf
to where it had been"""),

("write a product description for a plain white coffee mug",
"""It holds twelve ounces of whatever gets you through the morning. Its walls are thick enough to keep coffee warm through a long first email and its handle is wide enough for a whole hand on a cold day. It's plain white, which means it will never clash with your kitchen, never date itself, and never compete with what's inside it. It's dishwasher and microwave safe, it stacks neatly, and it has the quiet confidence of an object that knows exactly what it's for. This is the mug you'll reach for first, every time, without quite knowing why."""),

("write a short love letter from the moon to the ocean",
"""My dear,

Every night I cross the sky only to look at you, and every night you rise to meet me and fall back again, as if you'd almost forgotten yourself. They call it gravity, these small creatures on your shores, and they measure it in tables and charts. Let them. I know it as the oldest conversation in the world. I cannot come closer, and you cannot leave the earth, so we have settled into this: twice a day you reach for me, and twice a day you let me go. Some nights I hide my face from you, but only so that the next time I return, full and bright, you'll lift a little higher to see me.

Yours, through every tide,
The Moon"""),

("write a scene where two strangers share an umbrella",
"""The rain came on all at once, the way it does in October, and by the time Theo reached the bus shelter it was already full. He stood at the edge with his collar up, water running down the back of his neck, until a woman beside him tilted her umbrella an inch in his direction without looking at him.

"You're getting wet," she said, to the street.

"I'd noticed," he said, and stepped under. The umbrella was small and patterned with lemons, and they had to stand closer than strangers usually stand. Neither of them said anything for a while. The rain drummed on the lemons.

"Number nine?" she asked, finally.

"Number nine."

"It's always late when it rains."

"Everything's late when it rains," Theo said, and she laughed, a short, surprised laugh, as if she hadn't expected to. When the bus came, they stepped out from the shelter together, and he noticed that she held the umbrella over both of them all the way to the door."""),

("describe a city at 5 in the morning",
"""At five in the morning the city belongs to the people who keep it running. Delivery trucks idle outside shuttered cafés while men in gloves stack crates of bread and milk on the curb. A street sweeper hums past, leaving a dark wet stripe down the middle of the avenue. The traffic lights cycle patiently through their colors for no one. In a few windows, a lamp is on: a nurse coming home, a baker going out, someone who never went to sleep. The air is cold and smells faintly of the river. Then, almost without anyone noticing, the sky behind the buildings turns from black to a deep blue, the first bus pulls away full of tired faces, and the city begins, once again, to belong to everyone."""),

("write a short story about a detective who is a dog",
"""Detective Biscuit had solved forty-one cases, all of them by smell, and none of them had ever been reported to the police, because the cases were mostly about who had eaten what. The forty-second began on a Tuesday, when the roast chicken vanished from the kitchen counter. The human was furious. The cat was calm, which was suspicious in itself. Biscuit worked the scene methodically: the counter, the floor beneath it, the trail of grease leading toward the laundry room. There, behind the dryer, he found the evidence, along with a strong scent of cat. He sat beside it and barked until the human came. The human looked at the chicken, then at Biscuit, then at the grease on Biscuit's whiskers, which had gotten there entirely by accident during the investigation. Biscuit was sent to the yard. The cat watched from the window, licking a paw. Some cases, he reflected, are solved; others merely closed."""),

("write a short speech for my coworker's retirement party",
"""When Linda joined this office thirty-two years ago, we had one computer, which nobody trusted, and a filing system that only Linda understood. The computers have multiplied since then. Linda's filing system, as far as I can tell, has never been fully explained to anyone, which may be why we're all so nervous about tomorrow.

What Linda gave this place wasn't just knowledge, though she had more of it than anyone. It was steadiness. In every crisis, and we've had a few, she was the one who said, "All right, let's see what we've got," and somehow we always had more than we thought.

Linda, you've spent three decades making the rest of us look organized. Now you get to spend your time on the garden, the grandchildren, and that trip to Portugal you've been planning since before some of us were hired. We'll miss you more than we can say. To Linda."""),

("write an opening paragraph for a fantasy novel",
"""The bells of Carrow rang for a death every evening at dusk, and had done so for three hundred years, though no one in the town could say whose death it was. Visitors assumed it was a custom and found it charming. The townspeople knew better than to ask. They simply paused at the first stroke, wherever they were, and waited for the twelfth before going on with their business. So when the bells rang thirteen times on the last night of autumn, every door on every street opened at once, and every face turned toward the tower, and in the silence that followed, a girl named Wren, who had been born in Carrow and had never once thought about the bells, realized that everyone was looking at her."""),

("write a limerick about a cat",
"""A cat with a taste for the grand
Insisted on being hand-fanned.
When the fanning grew slow,
She would sigh and say, "Oh,
The help in this house is so bland." """),

("describe nostalgia without using the word",
"""It's the ache that comes from a smell in a stairwell that matches a stairwell you climbed at nine years old, or from a song on a café radio that you haven't heard since the summer everything changed. It's warm and sore at the same time, like pressing on a bruise that has almost healed. You find yourself missing not just a place or a person but a version of yourself who didn't know what was coming, and who, you suspect now, was happier than they realized. You can't go back, and you wouldn't truly want to. But for a moment, you'd give a great deal to stand in that kitchen again and hear someone call you in for dinner."""),

("write a short story about the last bookstore on mars",
"""The last bookstore on Mars stood at the end of a pressurized arcade in Tharsis City, between a hydroponics vendor and a shop that repaired air filters. Its owner, Okonkwo, had shipped every volume from Earth at enormous cost, back when shipping was still cheap enough to be merely ruinous. Now the colonists read on their visors, and most came in only to touch the paper, which they did reverently, as if it were soil from home. A boy came in every week to read the same book about oceans. He'd never seen one. Okonkwo let him read for free, standing in the aisle under the amber dome lights, while dust storms scoured the windows. When the boy turned sixteen and earned a berth on the first water-prospecting ship to the northern ice, he bought the book at last, with his first paycheck. Okonkwo wrapped it in recycled foil and didn't charge him for the wrapping."""),

("write a postcard from a vacation in iceland",
"""Greetings from the end of the world, or at least it feels that way. Yesterday we drove for hours along the south coast past waterfalls that pour straight off the cliffs, and black sand beaches where the waves come in like they mean it. The landscape looks unfinished, like the planet hasn't decided what it wants to be yet: lava fields covered in thick green moss, glaciers spilling down between mountains, steam rising from cracks in the ground. Tonight we're staying in a small guesthouse and hoping for the northern lights. The sun barely sets, the wind never stops, and the hot springs are worth every cold minute it takes to get into them. Wish you were here. Wear a hat if you ever come."""),

("write a eulogy for my houseplant, make it funny",
"""We are gathered here today to remember Gerald, a fern of uncommon patience and limited resilience. Gerald came to us from the clearance shelf at a garden center, already slightly brown at the edges, and we chose to see that as character.

Gerald enjoyed sunlight in theory, water in moderation, and being moved to a new location every three days in the hope that this would help. It did not. He endured overwatering in March, underwatering in June, and a brief but intense period of encouragement in which he was spoken to daily and played classical music. He responded to all of it the same way: by dropping one more leaf, quietly, as if to make a point.

Gerald is survived by a pot, a saucer, and a cactus who outlived him out of sheer spite. In lieu of flowers, please do not send flowers. We cannot be trusted."""),

("write a dialogue between a grumpy chef and a cheerful waiter",
"""WAITER: Table six loved the soup! They said it was the best soup they've ever had!

CHEF: They're wrong.

WAITER: They asked for the recipe.

CHEF: Tell them it died with my grandmother.

WAITER: Your grandmother's in the dining room right now. She's at table four.

CHEF: Then tell her to keep quiet about it.

WAITER: Also, table nine wants to know if the fish is fresh.

CHEF: The fish was swimming this morning, which is more than I can say for table nine.

WAITER: I'll tell them yes!

CHEF: Tell them whatever makes them stop asking.

WAITER: You know, Chef, I think deep down you love this job.

CHEF: Deep down, I love silence. Take the plates before they get cold."""),

("write a short story about a girl who can hear colors",
"""Lena discovered at six that other people couldn't hear the colors, and she was so shocked that she didn't speak about it again for years. To her, red was a low brass note, warm and a little pushy; yellow was a bright, high whistle; blue was a cello holding one long tone. A field of poppies sounded like a marching band. Her grandmother's green sweater hummed like a choir far away. Hospitals were hard, with their humming white walls, and so were supermarkets, where the cereal aisle shrieked. But at dusk, when the sky moved slowly from gold through rose to deep violet, the world played a chord so beautiful that she had to sit down. When she finally told her art teacher, at fifteen, the teacher didn't laugh. She handed Lena a brush and said, "Then paint me something I can hear." """),

("write a gracious resignation letter",
"""Dear Ms. Alvarez,

Please accept this letter as formal notice of my resignation from my position as project coordinator. My last day will be Friday, March 14, two weeks from today.

This was not an easy decision. I've learned a great deal here over the past three years, and much of that is thanks to your guidance and the trust you've placed in me. I'm especially grateful for the chance to lead the regional rollout, which taught me more than any job I've had.

I'll do everything I can to make the transition smooth, including documenting my current projects and helping to train whoever takes them on. Please let me know how I can best help in the coming weeks.

Thank you again for everything. I hope our paths cross again.

Sincerely,
Jordan Lee"""),

("describe a forest after snowfall",
"""After the snow, the forest goes silent in a way that feels deliberate, as if every sound has been gathered up and put away. The trees stand in white coats, their branches bowed under ridges of snow that occasionally slip loose and fall with a soft, heavy hush. The light is strange and even, coming from everywhere at once, so that there are no shadows to speak of, only shades of white and gray and the dark lines of trunks. Your own footsteps sound enormous. Every so often you find the neat track of a fox or the tiny scattered prints of a bird, and you realize that the forest has been busy all night, while you slept, writing in a language only the morning can read."""),

("write a six word story",
"""Her umbrella, still by my door."""),

("write a short sci-fi story about first contact",
"""When the signal came, it was not a message but a question, repeated every eleven minutes from a point just beyond Neptune: a sequence of pulses that, after three weeks of work, the linguists translated as "Are you lonely too?" The world argued about how to answer. Governments drafted statements; philosophers wrote essays; a committee spent a month debating whether to include mathematics. In the end, a radio astronomer in Chile, working the night shift alone, typed a reply before anyone could stop her. She sent a single word, encoded the same way the question had been. "Yes." The pulses stopped. For six hours, the whole planet held its breath. Then the signal returned, slightly changed, and the linguists needed only a moment to translate it. "Good. So are we. Let's talk." """),

("write a scene in a diner at midnight",
"""The diner sat at the edge of the highway like a lantern someone had forgotten to put out. Inside, the fluorescent lights buzzed over six empty booths and a counter where a trucker worked through a plate of eggs with the steady rhythm of a man who had many miles left. The waitress, Dolores, according to her name tag and probably also in fact, refilled his coffee without being asked. In the corner booth, a young couple in wedding clothes sat across from each other sharing a slice of cherry pie, the bride's veil folded on the seat beside her like a sleeping animal. Nobody asked them about it. At midnight in a diner, everyone is somewhere between one thing and the next, and the kindest thing you can do is keep the coffee warm."""),

("write an apology letter from a raccoon who raided my trash",
"""Dear Resident of the Blue House,

I am writing to apologize for the events of Tuesday night. I want you to know that I did not set out to make a mess. I set out to have dinner, and the mess was simply what happened afterward, as it so often does.

In my defense, the lid was not fully closed. In your defense, it was closed enough that a reasonable raccoon would have moved on. I am not a reasonable raccoon. I have children.

I regret the coffee grounds on your driveway, the chicken bones on your lawn, and the yogurt container I carried to your porch for reasons I cannot fully explain. I do not regret the leftover lasagna. It was excellent, and I hope you will make it again.

With sincere apologies and cautious hope,
The Raccoon (you know which one)"""),

("describe the ocean to someone who has never seen it",
"""Imagine a field that moves. It stretches flat to the edge of the sky, so wide that you can see the curve of the earth in it, and its whole surface is alive with slow, rolling shapes, as if something enormous were breathing underneath. It's blue and gray and green, changing color with the sky and the hour. At the shore it rises into waves that fold over themselves with a roar, then slide up the sand in thin, glassy sheets and pull back with a sound like a long exhale. It smells of salt and seaweed and wind. Standing in front of it, people tend to go quiet. It's the largest thing most of us will ever see, and it makes you feel small in a way that is, somehow, a relief."""),

("write a short myth explaining why owls hoot at night",
"""Long ago, when the world was new, the Sun asked all the birds to help her keep watch over the earth. Each bird chose a task. The rooster promised to call her up each morning; the lark promised to sing her across the sky; the swallow promised to follow her west each evening. Owl came last, and all the daytime tasks were taken. "Then I will keep watch while you sleep," Owl said. The Sun was moved, and she gave Owl her own great round eyes, so that he could see in the dark. But she had nothing left to give him to sing with. So each night, Owl calls out across the forest, "Who? Who?" asking every shadow whether it belongs there, and when the answer is silence, he knows the world is safe until morning."""),

("write a short funny story about a time traveler who just wants a sandwich",
"""Dr. Elena Voss built the first working time machine in her garage, and for her maiden voyage she chose, after much reflection, to go back eleven minutes. She had left a sandwich on the counter before starting the final calibration, and she was hungry. She arrived to find her past self eating it. They looked at each other. "I'll make another," said past Elena. "There's no more bread," said present Elena, who knew. They stood in silence. Then they both looked at the time machine, and both had the same idea at once, and both reached for the controls. The resulting paradox briefly erased the entire garage, the sandwich, and Tuesday. When the universe stabilized, Elena was standing in a field, holding half a sandwich, with no memory of how she'd gotten there. She ate it anyway. It was, she later wrote in her notes, an excellent sandwich, and almost worth it."""),

("write a short encouraging message for my friend who's running a marathon tomorrow",
"""Tomorrow you get to run the race you've been training for all these months, and every early alarm, every long Sunday run, every sore morning is already packed into your legs. You don't have to find the strength tomorrow; you've been building it all along. Start slower than you want to, drink at the stations, and when it gets hard around mile twenty, remember that it's supposed to, and that hard is not the same as impossible. I'll be thinking of you at every mile, and I'll be at the finish line, ready to be ridiculously proud. Go get it."""),
]
